DA-1: INV-003 human_review, decision agent (claude-opus-5-5, definition 3dd46b53…1219, revision 12), for TASK-070 at commit d946b012457303c9b784c275524f53a2c4fb97f2.
- Article: INV-003 (constitution.md:37-40). "The bootstrap implementation is disposable and must not become an accidental second core"; check: judgment; evidence_required: [human_review].
- Inputs I read myself, in SP/rev/in/t70b (copied under D-1567; each file's SHA-256 is worker-supplied, from finish_t70b.out:2-39):
  - now/session_violation.py, in full (151 lines);
  - TASK-070.yaml, in full;
  - t070-change.diff: its headers, its hunk header and a Grep for removed lines;
  - ctx/base-adapter.py :60-104 and :210-300;
  - now/test_session_violation.py :352-401;
  - ac7run70.out and ci70b3.txt, in full; brk3.part41-47.txt:17;
  - review70-R1, in full; R2 to R5, by Grep for their outcomes.
- Findings:
  - No second core. session_violation.py composes existing functions and judges nothing that the core already judges:
    - the lookup is writer.session_lease, the default reached at call time (:123);
    - the record, the fence and the release are adapter.violation → violation.record → the writer (:135-136), called unchanged.
    It does not re-implement the fence, the record, the observation check or the release. It does no token check of its own against the lease; a broken copy that adds one is required to be caught (TASK-070.yaml:628-630). Its own logic is limited to:
    - argument kinds (:54-62);
    - the shape check of a tied lease (:65-74);
    - the output-side removal of tokens and the copying of the answer (:77-113);
    - fail-closed endings (:116-151).
    The token removal is a new duty. It only narrows what leaves the core, so it adds no authority. The module keeps no state between calls: its four module-level values (:48-51) are a frozenset and str constants.
  - One channel to the store and the log. The imports are exactly aieos_bootstrap.adapter and aieos_bootstrap.writer (:45-46).
    - Of writer, the module uses only session_lease and StoreResult.
    - It opens no file and reads no clock, environment or standard input.
    - The AST test pins these facts (test_session_violation.py:367-391): the import set equals {adapter, writer}, the writer attributes used equal {session_lease, StoreResult}, there is no open, exec, eval, compile, __import__ or importlib, no clock or environment attribute, no assert, and no "eventlog" in the code part.
    - writer.py stays the only opener: it is not in the diff.
  - Disposable. The module is a leaf.
    - Nothing in src imports it. It is new at this head (ac7run70.out:1), and the diff changes no other src path.
    - No hook-side module names it, and a test pins this over the six hook-side modules (test_session_violation.py:393-398).
    - Nothing wires it in: nothing under .claude/ or .github/, no setting and no matcher. Its caller is H2's PreToolUse program, a later contract (session_violation.py:41-42; TASK-070.yaml:57-60).
    - Removing it, together with its two new test modules and the added SessionViolationFiles class, leaves the rest of the bootstrap unchanged. adapter.py is byte-identical (ac7run70.out:5).
  - Coupling to adapter's private helpers (_result, _text, _utc_whole, _task, _token_form). This is a dependency of the new leaf on adapter, not the other way round, so it does not make adapter depend on the new code. The CODE_AT_BASE pins keep adapter.py from changing under it (TASK-070.yaml:47-49). Not an INV-003 issue; R1:24 agrees.
  - A point of my own (into H2's hardening item, beside D-1569 C1):
    - (i) The contract says the lookup's answer "is read through adapter._store_done" (TASK-070.yaml:69) and lists _store_done among the helpers used (:48, :263). The module does not use _store_done. It reads answer.result, answer.reason and answer.findings directly, after an isinstance test for StoreResult (session_violation.py:67, :130-131).
    - The effect is fail-closed on the real path: the writer's StoreResult is always well formed. A malformed answer ends as "lease", or as "entry" through the outer guard; the latter is D-1569 C1 (c)'s case.
    - So this is a wording departure of the delivered code from its contract header, not a defect. It is named here and goes to the acceptance record and to H2's hardening item.
- Carried limits: the header's stated limits (TASK-070.yaml:130-161), in particular:
  - the race between the lookup and the appends;
  - the partial outcome and the same-event_id retry;
  - "entry: after the record";
  - the session tie and now as the caller's (the fail-open direction of an early now);
  - the observed read-set record left for point 10;
  - C6 and P-LOC unmeasured; nothing placed, wired, set or measured;
  - DEF-0084 h4c Y1-7 still open for H2.
- The implementer's claims, not read results:
  - the local runs, the 47 broken copies and the AC7 runs (I read their outputs, not the runs);
  - the pre-scans;
  - that the files in now/ and the diff equal d946b012;
  - every SHA-256.
- Outcome: pass. INV-003 holds. The change adds one thin, stateless, unwired leaf module. It composes the existing lookup and the existing violation path, and reaches the store and the log only through the single writer, by way of adapter.violation and violation.record. Its own logic only narrows what leaves the core.
